{% comment %}
  Appx — Product Specs Table
  Resolves the current product to its assigned template HANDLE from the shop-level
  routing map + this product's routing shard (feature 43 + feature 108,
  context/features/43-storefront-routing-resolution.md,
  context/features/108-routing-metaobject-sharding.md).

  Params (render scope is isolated — all passed explicitly):
  - product: the current product.
  - routing: the parsed `shop.metafields["$app"].routing.value` json object, in the
             COMPACT delivery shape written by `compactRoutingForDelivery`
             (app/utils/routingProjection.ts). WIRE v3 — BROAD TIERS ONLY:
               { v, handles[], def, byType, byVendor, byCollection }
             Every by*/def value is an INDEX into `handles`. This is a private wire
             contract with that function; the two move together.
  - shard:   THIS product's `$app:appx_routing_shard` metaobject (feature 108),
             resolved and passed in by `blocks/spec_table.liquid` as
             `metaobjects["$app:appx_routing_shard"]["routing-shard-<product.id mod N>"]`.
             Two json fields, read as `.value`:
               by_product : { "<product id>": "<template handle STRING>" }  (D3)
               excluded   : { "<product id>": 1 }                           (membership)
             The per-product maps that used to sit in `routing.byProduct` /
             `routing.excluded` moved HERE, so each product page reads only its own
             small shard instead of the whole map. A product in a sparse (unoccupied)
             bucket gets `shard == nil`, so both lookups are nil — a clean miss that
             falls through to the broad tiers.

  Emits: the resolved template handle STRING, or nothing. Liquid render scope is
  isolated (a snippet cannot return a value), so the block CAPTURES this output.

  Resolution order (data-model §9, feature 45 Decision B). The ACTIVE rule set is
  disjoint (feature 42), so at most one key matches — the order is efficiency, never
  precedence. An EXPLICIT single-product assignment (`shard.by_product`) is checked
  BEFORE the exclude gate, so an excluded product still reaches its own dedicated table
  (the "all products EXCEPT X, and X gets its own table" story); the exclude gate
  (`shard.excluded`) then only carves products out of the BROAD tiers:
    shard.by_product[<product id>]
      -> shard.excluded? -> nothing
      -> byType[type] -> byVendor[vendor]
        -> byCollection[<collection id>] (scan) -> def
  The per-product tier resolves to a handle STRING directly (D3 — shards store handles,
  not indices); each BROAD tier resolves to a handle INDEX (`hidx`) dereferenced through
  `routing.handles` once, only if the exclude gate let us in. This is now the ONLY source
  of an assignment. The per-product override metafield that used to sit ahead of it in the
  block was removed 2026-08-04 (see `blocks/spec_table.liquid`).

  SHAPE NOTES:
  - Keys are the BARE numeric id, so we build `'' | append: product.id` (forces a
    string key to match the JSON object keys) — NOT a `gid://...` token. The same
    `'' | append:` idiom builds the collection key.
  - A BROAD tier lookup returns a handle INDEX. The test is `!= blank`, never truthiness:
    index 0 is a valid handle and `0 == blank` is false in Liquid, but an absent key
    is nil (blank). Resolving to an index first and dereferencing `handles` once guards
    against `handles[nil]` coercing nil -> 0 and returning the wrong table on a miss.
    The per-product tier needs no such guard — `shard.by_product.value[pid]` is already
    the final handle string (or nil).
  - byType / byVendor keys are raw selector strings (a blank product type/vendor
    indexes a key that never exists, so it falls through). TAG routing is intentionally
    NOT read — the compact shape omits `byTag` entirely (post-MVP; always empty).

  Collections are scanned in 50-item chunks (Shopify caps `for` at 50), breaking
  on the first hit — mirroring the block's rows loop.
{% endcomment %}
{%- liquid
  assign hidx = ''
  assign handle = ''

  if routing != blank
    assign pid = '' | append: product.id

    # An explicit single-product assignment wins even for an excluded product
    # (feature 45 Decision B) — it is checked BEFORE the exclude gate. Sourced from
    # this product's shard (feature 108); the value is the final handle STRING (D3).
    assign handle = shard.by_product.value[pid]

    # The broad tiers are carved out by the shard's `excluded` set: an excluded product
    # matches none of them (renders nothing unless byProduct claimed it above).
    if handle == blank
      unless shard.excluded.value[pid] != blank
        assign hidx = routing.byType[product.type]

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.byVendor[product.vendor]
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign coll_chunks = product.collections.size | plus: 49 | divided_by: 50
          for chunk in (1..coll_chunks)
            assign coll_offset = chunk | minus: 1 | times: 50
            for collection in product.collections offset: coll_offset limit: 50
              assign cid = '' | append: collection.id
              assign coll_hit = routing.byCollection[cid]
              if coll_hit != blank
                assign hidx = coll_hit
                break
              endif
            endfor
            if hidx != blank
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.def
        endif

        # Dereference the broad-tier handle table ONCE, guarded so a miss (nil hidx)
        # never indexes handles[0] by nil-coercion.
        if hidx != blank
          assign handle = routing.handles[hidx]
        endif
      endunless
    endif
  endif

  echo handle
-%}
